import lib/process.dl

to get [the|] partial write test interpreter:
    return "python" if the process platform is Windows, else "python3"

set command to a process command for the partial write test interpreter
add "-u" as an argument to command
add "-c" as an argument to command
add "import sys,time;time.sleep(0.1);data=sys.stdin.buffer.read();sys.stdout.write(str(len(data)));sys.stdout.flush()" as an argument to command
launch command and set child to it

wait up to 10 milliseconds for process activity from child and set idle to it
print line (idle succeeded) and (idle reports no activity)

set input to a new string buffer containing 1048576 bytes
write input to the standard input of child and set first to it
print lines (first succeeded) and (first's byte count > (0 as a word sized integer))
print line (first's byte count < (the length of input as a word sized integer))

set written to first's byte count as an integer
loop while written < the length of input:
    set remaining to the substring of input from written spanning ((the length of input) - written) bytes
    write remaining to the standard input of child and set outcome to it
    if (outcome failed) or (outcome's byte count = (0 as a word sized integer)):
        print line false
        @intrinsic("call", "libc", "abort", nothing)
    set chunk to outcome's byte count as an integer
    set written to (written + chunk)

close the standard input of child and set closed to it
print line closed succeeded
wait up to 2000 milliseconds for process activity from child and set ready to it
print line (ready succeeded) and (ready reports activity)
read the standard output of child and set output to it
print line (output's contents)
wait up to 2000 milliseconds for child and set finished to it
print line (finished succeeded) and (finished reports completion)
clean up child and set cleaned to it
print line cleaned succeeded
